<LegalLayout title="Centrix Security and Vulnerability Reporting" description="How to report vulnerabilities, exposed information or security issues to Centrix.">
  <p>If you believe you have found a vulnerability, exposed information or another security issue affecting Centrix, report it privately to <a href="mailto:security@centrix.ie">security@centrix.ie</a>. We welcome clear reports that help us protect people and systems.</p>
  <p>Centrix is based at Downestown, Duleek, Co. Meath, Ireland. For privacy rights requests use <a href="mailto:data@centrix.ie">data@centrix.ie</a>; for general enquiries use <a href="mailto:info@centrix.ie">info@centrix.ie</a>.</p>
  <h2>1 What to report</h2>
  <p>Use this reporting route for suspected vulnerabilities in centrix.ie or other systems operated and controlled by Centrix; exposed credentials or confidential information relating to Centrix; and suspected compromise, phishing or impersonation involving Centrix.</p>
  <p>Client-owned systems and third-party services are not automatically within Centrix’s authority. Reports can be referred to the appropriate owner where relevant, but this policy does not authorise testing those systems.</p>
  <h2>2 How to send a useful report</h2>
  <p>Email <a href="mailto:security@centrix.ie">security@centrix.ie</a> with the subject “Security report” and a short description. If you believe an incident is actively occurring, state that clearly. Include, where safely available:</p>
  <p>The affected URL, hostname or service and when you observed the issue, including time zone.</p>
  <p>A description of the issue, the likely impact and the minimum steps needed to reproduce it.</p>
  <p>A sanitised screenshot, relevant error message or limited proof of concept that avoids personal data and secrets.</p>
  <p>Your preferred contact method and whether you want your identity kept private.</p>
  <p>Do not email passwords, private keys, authentication tokens, full databases or unnecessary personal data. Ask us to arrange a secure transfer method before sending sensitive evidence. Do not post the issue in a public repository, social media thread or ordinary website contact form.</p>
  <h2>3 Safe boundaries</h2>
  <p>Stop once you have enough information to describe a potential issue. If you unexpectedly encounter personal or confidential data, do not explore further, download more information, alter it or share it. Report what happened using minimal details.</p>
  <p>Do not conduct denial-of-service activity, high-volume scanning, credential stuffing, phishing, social engineering, persistence, destructive testing or access to other people’s accounts. Do not install malware or use an issue to obtain services, money or additional access. Ask for written authorisation before any testing beyond ordinary permitted use.</p>
  <h2>4 How we handle reports</h2>
  <p>We aim to acknowledge reports promptly, assess the affected system and potential impact, and prioritise the response according to risk. We may ask for clarification or coordinate with a client or provider where the issue concerns their system.</p>
  <p>Where you provide a contact method, we will seek to keep you informed of material progress. Investigation and remediation times depend on the issue, dependencies and available evidence. This mailbox is not a guaranteed continuously monitored incident response or emergency support service. Existing clients should also use their agreed incident escalation route.</p>
  <h2>5 Coordinated disclosure</h2>
  <p>Please give us a reasonable opportunity to investigate and address the issue before publishing technical details. We will discuss an appropriate disclosure date with you, taking account of risk, affected parties and remediation progress. Please do not disclose personal data, credentials or details that would unnecessarily expose users to harm.</p>
  <p>We will not publicly identify you as a reporter without your agreement, unless disclosure is legally required. Attribution can be discussed where appropriate. Reporting does not create an entitlement to payment, a bounty, a contract or a reward.</p>
  <h2>6 Authorisation and lawful reporting</h2>
  <p>This page provides a reporting process. It is not permission to access systems without authority or to carry out intrusive testing, and it cannot grant permission on behalf of another organisation. Any authorised assessment must have a separately agreed scope and written approval from the relevant system owner.</p>
  <p>Nothing in this policy prevents a lawful report to a regulator or law enforcement, or removes protections available under applicable law.</p>
  <h2>7 Information in your report</h2>
  <p>We use report information to investigate, secure systems, communicate with you and meet applicable legal duties. Access is limited to people who need it, which may include relevant providers, affected clients, advisers or authorities where necessary. Our <a href="/privacy">Privacy Notice</a> explains our legal bases, providers, international processing and your rights.</p>
  <p>Report records are normally kept for three years after closure. Sensitive evidence is deleted sooner when no longer needed. A legal obligation, investigation or claim may require a documented extension. Privacy enquiries about a report can be sent to <a href="mailto:data@centrix.ie">data@centrix.ie</a>.</p>
  <h2>8 Policy updates</h2>
  <p>We may update this reporting process as our services change. The date above identifies the current version. For security reports, use <a href="mailto:security@centrix.ie">security@centrix.ie</a>.</p>
</LegalLayout>
